# Modern Native C++ (C++20/23) Engineering Standards

This skill provides advanced guidelines, idioms, and high-performance patterns for developing mission-critical, low-latency, and memory-safe native systems in **C++20 / C++23**.

---

## 1. Modern C++ Core Idioms & RAII

### 1.1 Custom Deleters with `std::unique_ptr`
Always encapsulate non-memory resources (Win32 handles, file descriptors, C libraries) in zero-overhead RAII wrappers:

```cpp
#include <memory>
#include <concepts>
#include <cstdio>

// Zero-overhead RAII handle wrapper
struct FileCloser {
    void operator()(std::FILE* fp) const noexcept {
        if (fp) std::fclose(fp);
    }
};
using UniqueFile = std::unique_ptr<std::FILE, FileCloser>;

template <typename T, auto DeleterFunc>
struct CustomDeleter {
    void operator()(T* ptr) const noexcept {
        if (ptr) DeleterFunc(ptr);
    }
};

template <typename T, auto DeleterFunc>
using ScopedNativeHandle = std::unique_ptr<T, CustomDeleter<T, DeleterFunc>>;
```

### 1.2 Concepts, Constraints & Error Handling (`std::expected`)
Eliminate runtime exceptions in performance-critical paths by employing `std::expected` (C++23) and compile-time concepts:

```cpp
#include <expected>
#include <string_view>
#include <concepts>

enum class SystemErrorCode {
    InvalidArgument,
    BufferOverflow,
    ResourceUnavailable,
    Timeout
};

template <typename T>
concept NumericData = std::integral<T> || std::floating_point<T>;

template <NumericData T>
[[nodiscard]] constexpr auto CalculateChecksum(std::span<const T> buffer) noexcept 
    -> std::expected<uint64_t, SystemErrorCode> 
{
    if (buffer.empty()) [[unlikely]] {
        return std::unexpected(SystemErrorCode::InvalidArgument);
    }

    uint64_t sum = 0;
    for (const auto& val : buffer) {
        sum += static_cast<uint64_t>(val);
    }
    return sum;
}
```

